{
  "request": {
    "model": "nomic-embed-text:latest",
    "input": [
      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nThe nurse reviews a commercial driver's symptoms alongside information from a witness, the treating team and an older workplace form, because each source describes a different part of the story; the occupational record retains those differences and identifies completed current findings without turning a booking, a family account or a recollection into a measured patient result.\n\nThe treating specialist confirms vasovagal syncope during venepuncture, identifies the well-defined provoking factor and explicitly assesses recurrence while driving as unlikely; the driver describes nausea and sweating before that observed faint, but has not had unprovoked events, and the nurse records the specialist's current assessment separately from a draft referral that had initially listed arrhythmia as an unconfirmed differential diagnosis.\n\nPart of the usual route requires reversing near workers and reading changing signs before entering a customer site, although today's assessment takes place in a quiet room; the driver is asked to describe the sequence and timing of difficulties, and the note preserves the difference between an occupational concern, a suspected mechanism and a completed clinical assessment.\n\nThe nurse keeps the relevant current attachment with the record and identifies the older account by its timing, so a later reviewer can distinguish what was observed, what the driver reported and what remains unresolved; no additional measurement, specialist conclusion or diagnosis is added merely because it would make the occupational decision easier to complete.\n"
    ],
    "truncate": false
  },
  "provider_called": true,
  "status": "completed",
